W21-HARNESS-FIGHTS-0904, closure-plan rows CL-H1 / CL-H2 (and CL-H7 in S07). Every fight in this segment was `press combat_quick, times: N` -- the failure mode SEGMENT_SCHEMA.md names by name, and the one G3-BAND2, G3-BAND3, G3-BAND4 and G3-BAND5 each derailed on in turn. A counted press block is right for exactly one matchup: a fight's length is a function of levels, the type chart and a +/-10% roll on every hit, and a press step only ever asserts that INPUT WAS INJECTED, so a block that ran out mid-fight (or emptied itself into a party wipe) reported PASS either way. Three changes, applied uniformly:
  1. every fight is now `fight_until_resolved` -- combat_quick only while the action machine reads READY, one party_cycle when the pilot drops below 35% of max HP, and a stop only when both is_fighting() and trainer_battle_active() have been quiet. A block that was a CATCH chip (S08's two wild engages) becomes `chip_to_floor` instead, which reads the target's real HP and deliberately does NOT resolve the fight;
  2. every challenge conversation is `advance_dialogue_until_closed` instead of a fixed press count. G3-BAND5 measured twelve presses running out before the outer watch's panel closed, leaving input_context on `narrative_modal` where the next step expected combat;
  3. every fight is preceded by a recovery ladder -- a Revive addressed BY ITEM IDENTITY (never a slot), then a switch -- ending in the `active_creature_alive` party-health gate, and followed by a bag-free post-faint switch. G3-BAND2 watched a fully fainted party walk 450 s into a dungeon and drop its whole satchel because nothing in the script ever recovered it.
Scripted mid-fight `party_cycle` beats are gone with the counted blocks that surrounded them: fight_until_resolved's own switch fires on the pilot's real HP, which is what CB-09's 'switching under pressure' means. Scripted `combat_charged` and `camera_recenter` presses are kept and moved to the fight's opening, because fight_until_resolved owns the fight end to end and nothing can be threaded through its middle.
